        "math_academy_id": "e-8688",
        "knowledge_point": "Reversing the Limits of Integration",
        "problem": "Calculate \n$\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\mathbf f(t) + \\langle 1,\\:-1,\\:1\\rangle\\,\\textrm{d}t$\ngiven that\n$\\displaystyle\\int_2^0\\mathbf f(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t= \\left\\langle -3, \\: 2, \\: 1 \\right\\rangle.$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\u27e8-3,\\,2,\\,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e83,\\,-2,\\,-1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the vector addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)+\u27e81,\\,-1,\\,1\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2}\u27e81,\\,-1,\\,1\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e83,\\,-2,\\,-1\u27e9+\u27e8t,\\,-t,\\,t\u27e9{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\u27e83,\\,-2,\\,-1\u27e9+\u27e82,\\,-2,\\,2\u27e9\\text{\u00a0} \\\\  & =\u27e85,\\,-4,\\,1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080120/example-8688.png"
      }
    ],
    "feedback": [
      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-98156",
        "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{2}^{3}[f(t)-3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e82,\\,-4,\\,-2\u27e9$\nand \n${\\int }_{2}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e81,\\,2,\\,-2\u27e9.$",
        "worked_solution": "From the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}[f(t)-3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-3{\\int }_{2}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}[f(t)-3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-3{\\int }_{2}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e82,\\,-4,\\,-2\u27e9-3\u27e81,\\,2,\\,-2\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82,\\,-4,\\,-2\u27e9-\u27e83,\\,6,\\,-6\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-1,\\,-10,\\,4\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
